<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Helping Around the House – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Wash the Dishes Everyday.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify main ideas on familiar topics in simple paragraphs. Reading: Illustrate main ideas from readings. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify the main ideas in simple paragraphs about household chores (e.g., "Everyone helps with chores."). Can illustrate main ideas from readings about helping at home, through drawings or discussions (e.g., "I help wash the dishes."). Can identify open and closed syllables in simple words (e.g., "do" as an open syllable and "cook" as a closed syllable).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>
            Activating prior knowledge and introducing the topic through engaging activities.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of different chores introduced in Lesson 1 (e.g., 'wash the dishes,' 'sweep the floor,' 'cook,' 'tidy up'). The teacher holds up a flashcard, and students chorally say the chore. Then, the teacher shows simple sentences written on the board or chart paper, such as "I clean the kitchen," "My family helps," or "We wash clothes." Students read the sentences aloud and point to the corresponding flashcard or a picture on the board. This connects prior vocabulary to simple reading.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading simple sentences about chores clearly, emphasizing words like "wash" /w/ and "sweep" /s/. For example, the teacher points to the sentence "I wash the dishes every day" and reads it aloud slowly, then points to "He sweeps the floor" and reads it. The teacher also models identifying the main idea by reading a simple sentence like "I like to help at home" and saying, "This sentence tells us that helping at home is good."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I reading a long story or a short sentence?" (Expected answer: A short sentence.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we listening or reading the words?" (Expected answer: Reading the words.)</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
            Introducing new language content through contextualized input materials.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated paragraph about a family helping at home. The text will use target vocabulary (dishes, house, chores, kitchen, family, wash, clean, cook, help) and grammar (Present simple, frequency adverb 'every day').</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple narrative excerpt titled "My Family Helps," printed on a large chart or individual handouts, with illustrations for key actions.
            <br><i>Example Text:</i>
            <br><i>"My family helps in our house every day. I wash the dishes after dinner. My brother sweeps the floor in the living room. My mom cooks delicious food in the kitchen. My dad cleans the bathroom. We all help to tidy up the house. Helping is important!"</i></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identifies main ideas on familiar topics in simple paragraphs.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks: "Who helps in the house?" (Expected answer: My family.) "What is the text about?" (Expected answer: Helping at home/chores.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences about the text and circle T (True) or F (False).
                    <br>1. The family cleans the house every day. (T/F)
                    <br>2. The brother washes the dishes. (T/F)
                    <br>3. Helping is important. (T/F)</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
            Focused practice on accuracy of target language and skills.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying main ideas and details in simple paragraphs. Grammar: Present simple, frequency adverbs (every day). Vocabulary: dishes, house, chores, kitchen, wash, sweep, cook, clean, tidy up. Phonemic Awareness: Recognition of closed and open syllables.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Main Idea Identification & Vocabulary):</b> Students re-read the "My Family Helps" text individually. The teacher gives them a worksheet. For each sentence in the text, students draw a line under the main verb (e.g., 'wash,' 'sweeps,' 'cooks,' 'cleans') and circle the noun that is the object (e.g., 'dishes,' 'floor,' 'food,' 'bathroom'). Then, students look at the first two sentences of the text: "My family helps in our house every day. I wash the dishes after dinner." The teacher asks: "What is the main idea of these two sentences? Is it 'Washing dishes' or 'Family helps at home'?" Students circle the correct main idea from two choices provided.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Reading for Detail & Syllables):</b> The teacher writes a few words from the text on the board: "do," "cook," "wash," "sweep," "clean," "floor." The teacher explains open and closed syllables. "Open syllable ends with a vowel sound (like 'do' /duː/)." "Closed syllable ends with a consonant sound (like 'cook' /kʊk/)." Students then read each word from the list and identify if it has an open or closed syllable by writing 'O' or 'C' next to it. They also practice pronouncing /w/ in "wash" and /s/ in "sweep" when reading the words aloud.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Reading Comprehension Questions):</b> In pairs, students receive a simple Q&A worksheet based on the "My Family Helps" text. One student reads a question (e.g., "What does the brother do?"), and the other student finds the answer in the text and reads it aloud (e.g., "My brother sweeps the floor."). They switch roles. This activity focuses on reading for specific information.</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
            Students use the language in a communicative and realistic task.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will read a new short paragraph about a different child helping at home and illustrate the main idea. This directly addresses the learning outcome of "Can illustrate main ideas from readings."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual reading and drawing activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a new short paragraph on a worksheet, for example:
                    <br><i>"Sofia helps her family every afternoon. She waters the plants in the living room. She also folds the laundry with her mom. Sofia likes to help with the chores in her house."</i></li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the paragraph silently first, then identify the main idea.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below the paragraph, there is a box for drawing. Students draw a picture that illustrates the main idea of the paragraph (e.g., Sofia helping with chores, watering plants, folding laundry).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can then share their drawings in small groups, briefly explaining what their drawing shows, linking it back to the main idea they read. For example, "This is Sofia. She helps her family."</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
            Evaluating learning and checking comprehension.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the drawing activity and review of student illustrations and short explanations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their illustrated worksheet from Stage 4. They verbally state the main idea they illustrated (e.g., "Sofia helps with chores.") and show their drawing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Main Idea Identification:</b> Does the illustration reflect the main idea of the paragraph?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Illustration:</b> Is the drawing clear and understandable?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary (optional verbal check):</b> Can the student use 1-2 target words to describe their drawing? (e.g., "Sofia waters the plants.")</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small piece of paper:
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What new thing did you read today about helping at home?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "Can you find a word in the text with an open syllable (like 'do') or a closed syllable (like 'cook') and write it?"</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>
            Reviewing the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What was easy for you to read today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "What was challenging when reading or drawing the main idea?"</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The students showed good comprehension of main ideas in simple paragraphs. They successfully identified vocabulary words and practiced pronunciation. The drawing activity was engaging and helped illustrate understanding. Next time, I could provide more diverse simple texts to practice main idea identification or incorporate more complex sentences for higher-level thinking within the A1.3 range. Scaffolds for students struggling with identifying main ideas included pre-reading questions and peer discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The reading comprehension skills developed today will serve as a foundation for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), where students will use the target vocabulary and present simple structures to discuss their own household chores and routines orally.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>